**Choice: what `viva instance/doctor <one word>` does when the word isn't an instance**

**Context.** You deleted `tapped`, then ran `viva instance/doctor tapped`. Instead of "no instance 'tapped'", you got the hello-world report. The known-issues entry blames `locate()`, but that's wrong. `locate()` only runs in the two-param form. The single-param fallthrough is in doctor's own `split`:

```js
// systems/ghost/trajectories/instance/doctor.js:23
// `doctor <target> <filter>` is unambiguous; `doctor <one>` is not. one param names the instance
// only if the record resolves it — otherwise it narrows the instance you are on.
const split = async (ctx) => {
  const [first, second] = ctx.signal.params ?? [];
  if (first === undefined) return {};
  if (second !== undefined) {
    const found = await locate(ctx, first);
    return found.mount ? { target: found.mount, filter: second } : found;
  }
  return await paladin.ledger.instances
    .resolve(first)
    .then((held) => ({ target: held.mount }), () => ({ filter: first }));
};
```

What happened:

```
doctor tapped
  split
    instances.resolve("tapped")   ✗ no record (deleted)
    → { filter: "tapped" }        ← falls through silently
  lifecycle.mount(paladin.instance) → the session instance, hello-world
  narrow(rows, "tapped")          → probably 0 env rows, but the rest of the report still prints
```

`doctor` is doing what it was written to do. The problem is that one bare word can mean two things: an instance name or a filter. `tapped` and `nlp` look the same, so the "refuse an exact-looking token" option in the entry has nothing to tell them apart.

**Option A: keep it.** No code change. `doctor nlp` keeps narrowing the current instance. A typo'd or deleted instance name keeps quietly showing the session instance's report.

**Option B: refuse only when the word matches nothing.** Decide by the result instead of by how the word looks. If the word isn't an instance and filters away every env row, it was almost certainly meant as an instance name.

```js
// systems/ghost/trajectories/instance/doctor.js:47
  const rows = narrow(paladin.check.environment(paladin.instance), filter);
  if (filter && !target && !rows.length) {
    return (ctx.effect = { error: `no instance '${filter}', and no env row matches it — viva instances/list` });
  }
```

`!target` only holds in the one-param form, since the two-param form always resolves a target first. So `doctor chess nlp` behaves exactly as before. One catch: the check runs after `lifecycle.mount`, so it still mounts the session instance before refusing.

**Option C: one word always means an instance.** Delete the fallthrough. `doctor <one>` goes through `locate()` like `use`, `delete` and `rename`, so you get the same "no instance" error they give. Filtering the current instance becomes explicit: `doctor . nlp` (`.` resolves the cwd path in `locate`).

```js
// systems/ghost/trajectories/instance/doctor.js:25
const split = async (ctx) => {
  const [first, second] = ctx.signal.params ?? [];
  if (first === undefined) return {};
  const found = await locate(ctx, first);
  return found.mount ? { target: found.mount, filter: second } : found;
};
```

**Assess.** C makes all four instance verbs read a word the same way, which removes the ambiguity entirely. It costs `doctor nlp` as a shorthand. It also brings `locate()`'s fuzzy `pick` into `doctor`: an unknown word partly matching a slug or path (the lens also matches `mount`) could pick a different instance, and in a pipe several matches throw. B keeps the shorthand but still mounts first, and it decides based on what the env rows happen to contain.

**My pick: C.** One word reads one way on every instance verb, and it removes the ambiguity rather than guessing around it.

